# Task 0 - Simple web stack

A user wants to open www.foobar.com in their browser:

1. The browser asks DNS for the IP address of www.foobar.com.
2. DNS answers with 8.8.8.8, the IP of our single server.
3. The browser sends an HTTPS request to 8.8.8.8 (TCP port 443).
4. Nginx receives the request. It serves static files itself and forwards
   dynamic requests to the application server.
5. The application server runs the code base, queries the MySQL database if it
   needs data, and builds the page.
6. The page goes back through Nginx to the user's browser.

## Infrastructure

- 1 server (IP 8.8.8.8) hosting everything below
- 1 web server: Nginx
- 1 application server
- 1 application files (code base)
- 1 database: MySQL
- 1 domain name: foobar.com, with a www record pointing to 8.8.8.8

## Specifics

- What is a server: a computer (physical or virtual) that is always on and
  provides a service (here, hosting a website) to other computers, called
  clients, over a network.
- Role of the domain name: it is a human-friendly name for an IP address.
  People remember foobar.com, not 8.8.8.8. DNS translates the name into the IP.
- Type of DNS record of www in www.foobar.com: an A record. It maps the name
  www.foobar.com to an IPv4 address (8.8.8.8).
- Role of the web server (Nginx): it accepts the HTTP/HTTPS requests from
  users, serves static content (images, CSS, JS) and passes dynamic requests
  to the application server, then sends the response back to the user.
- Role of the application server: it executes the application code (business
  logic), generates dynamic content and talks to the database.
- Role of the database (MySQL): it stores and retrieves the website's data
  (users, articles, orders...) in a structured and persistent way.
- Communication with the user's computer: the server uses the HTTP protocol
  (HTTPS when encrypted) over TCP/IP.

## Issues with this infrastructure

- SPOF (single point of failure): there is only one server. If it crashes
  (hardware, network, a component like Nginx or MySQL), the whole website is
  down.
- Downtime during maintenance: deploying new code or updating a component
  often requires restarting the web server or application server, and the
  website is unavailable during the restart.
- Cannot scale: a single server has limited CPU, RAM and bandwidth. If there is
  too much incoming traffic, it becomes slow or crashes, and there is no other
  server to share the load.
